We will build a simple **"Task Manager"** app where you can add tasks and view them.

### Prerequisites
*   Node.js installed on your computer.
*   A code editor (VS Code recommended).

---

### Part 1: Project Structure
Create a folder named `my-app` and set up two subfolders: `client` (React) and `server` (Node/Express).

```text
my-app/
├── client/
│   ├── src/
│   │   ├── App.js
│   │   └── index.js
│   └── package.json
├── server/
│   ├── index.js
│   └── package.json
└── README.md
```

---

### Part 2: The Backend (Node & Express)

1.  Open your terminal, navigate to the `server` folder, and initialize the project:
    ```bash
    cd server
    npm init -y
    ```

2.  Install dependencies:
    ```bash
    npm install express cors nodemon
    ```
    *   `express`: Web framework.
    *   `cors`: Allows the React frontend to talk to the Node backend.
    *   `nodemon`: Auto-restarts the server when you change code (great for development).

3.  Create `server/index.js`:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const app = express();
const PORT = 5000;

// Middleware
app.use(cors()); // Allow React to access this API
app.use(express.json()); // Parse JSON bodies

// In-memory Database (Dummy Data)
let tasks = [
    { id: 1, text: "Buy groceries", completed: false },
    { id: 2, text: "Learn React", completed: true }
];

// API Routes

// 1. Get all tasks
app.get('/api/tasks', (req, res) => {
    res.json(tasks);
});

// 2. Add a new task
app.post('/api/tasks', (req, res) => {
    const { text } = req.body;
    if (!text) {
        return res.status(400).json({ error: "Text is required" });
    }

    const newTask = {
        id: Date.now(),
        text: text,
        completed: false
    };

    tasks.push(newTask);
    res.status(201).json(newTask);
});

// Start Server
app.listen(PORT, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

4.  Update `server/package.json` to add a start script:
    ```json
    "scripts": {
