To share data between the content script and the popup script, you can use the chrome.runtime.sendMessage API. Here's an example of how you can do this:

In popup.js, you can send a message to the content script using chrome.runtime.sendMessage:

chrome.runtime.sendMessage({action: 'get_token'}, function(response) {
// Handle the response from the content script
});

In the content script, you can listen for messages from the popup script using chrome.runtime.onMessage:

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(function(request, sender, sendResponse) {
if (request.action === 'get_token') {
// Send the token back to the popup script
sendResponse({token: 'your_token_value'});
}
});

This way, the content script can send the token data to the popup script, and the popup script can use it as needed.

Note that you need to make sure that the content script and popup script are properly registered in your manifest.json file, and that they have the necessary permissions to communicate with each other.

Also, make sure to handle errors and edge cases appropriately in your code.

